  "narrative": "The newly released independent validation for H24 is decisive. In high screen-quality screens, the KEAP1-loss association with weaker dependency_NFE2L2 has signed estimate +0.799 with independent 95% interval +0.759 to +0.839, using the stricter validation alpha. This is orders of magnitude larger than any plausible 10%-relative threshold for NFE2L2-scale outcomes and lies far from both the null and any meaningful opposite-direction effect. Thus H24 is accepted and closed: the KEAP1-loss effect is robust even when restricted to high-quality screens, and the independent validation interval supports the same huge positive signed association. Because this validates the high-ROC restricted contrast, I also close the broader accepted main effect H17; the original huge association remains accepted and now has supportive independent evidence, but further investigation of this main effect would be less informative than testing new predictors or specificity probes.\n\nFor the overdue response deadline on H3, the independent validation changes the conclusion from unresolved to reject. Discovery had signed estimate +0.0252 with interval -0.0003 to +0.0507, barely near the approximately 0.025 absolute threshold for a 10% effect on dependency_EGFR and still including null. Independent validation gave signed estimate -0.0060 with interval -0.0497 to +0.0377, i.e., a small opposite-direction point estimate and wide uncertainty. The combination does not support a clinically meaningful EGFR-amplification association with stronger dependency_EGFR. I therefore reject and close H3 and also close the adenocarcinoma refinement H35 as low priority, because the main EGFR-amplification effect is not supported.\n\nThe three major accepted dataset associations are now sufficiently characterized for the same dataset. SMARCA4-loss versus stronger dependency_SMARCA2 remains accepted, with huge direct estimates and robustness across Avana, non-Avana, high screen-quality, and high-Cas9 subsets. MSI-high versus stronger dependency_WRN remains accepted, with huge direct estimates and robustness across high ROC and high Cas9 subsets; the non-Avana WRN cell was expected sparse and is not needed for closure. KEAP1-loss versus weaker dependency_NFE2L2 remains accepted, with robustness across library, screen quality, Cas9 activity, non-Avana screens, and now independent validation of the high-ROC restricted estimate. I close the main accepted effects H6, H8, and H17 to focus future analysis on unresolved predictors and specificity probes rather than repeatedly validating effects that are already far above the clinical threshold.\n\nI also close H33, the ARID1A-loss interaction modifying the SMARCA4-loss effect on dependency_SMARCA2, because the direct ARID1A-positive probe H39 is the better next test and the interaction estimate +0.037 was imprecise and near the SMARCA2-scale 10% threshold. The current best next set of discovery analyses is the active unrun probe set: EGFR amplification versus TMED10 dependency, TP53 loss versus RPL11 dependency, marker-null direct controls, PIK3CA mutation versus STAT3 dependency, SMARCA4-loss effect restricted to ARID1A-loss lines, KRAS and ERBB2 subtype probes, TP53 loss versus USP7 dependency, BRAF V600E versus RIT1 dependency, and marker F versus NFE2L2 dependency. All conclusions remain associations within the supplied lung-lineage CRISPR dataset, not causal claims or claims of external generalizability."
